# Does the record support "an INVISIBLE monitoring relation is more dangerous than a VISIBLE one"?

**Verdict up front: ASSERTED by industry and by law/policy, ADJACENTLY MEASURED in two narrow places, DIRECTLY UNTESTED.** No study I reached compares harm from a covert monitoring relation against the *same* relation made visible.

---

## 1. DULT — the formal industry commitment

**Announcement:** Apple/Google, **2 May 2023** — https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2023/05/apple-google-partner-on-an-industry-specification-to-address-unwanted-tracking/ — submitted as an IETF Internet-Draft, three-month public comment period, commitment to ship production implementations by end of 2023. Named supporters: **Samsung, Tile, Chipolo, eufy Security, Pebblebee**. Quoted: Ron Huang (Apple VP Sensing & Connectivity), Dave Burke (Google VP Eng Android), **Erica Olsen (NNEDV)**, Alexandra Reeve Givens (CDT). Google's own May-2023 post: **NOT REACHED** (blog.google slugs 404; sitemap has no matching entry).

**Individual draft** `draft-detecting-unwanted-location-trackers` — https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-detecting-unwanted-location-trackers/
- **-00: 2023-05-02** · **-01: 2023-12-20** · **expired 2024-06-22**. 37 pages.
- Authors: **Ben Detwiler (Apple), Brent Ledvina (Apple), Siddika Polatkan (Google), Zachary Eddinger (Google)**.
- Five Apple IPR disclosures 2023-05-04, five more 2023-10-19. Sessions at IETF-117 (Jul 2023) and IETF-118 BoF (Nov 2023).

**Working group** `dult`, Security Area — https://datatracker.ietf.org/wg/dult/about/
- Charter -00-00 2023-07-11 → external review 2024-02-16 → **IESG approved 2024-02-29**.
- Chairs **Erica Olsen (NNEDV)** and **Sean Turner**. AD: Danyliw → Deb Cooley (2025-04-09) → **Christopher Inacio (2026-03-18)**.
- Both charter milestones due **2025-07-31**. Neither met.

**Status as of 2026-09-04 — no RFC published.**

| Draft | Rev | Last posted | State |
|---|---|---|---|
| `draft-ietf-dult-accessory-protocol` | **00** | 2024-11-03 (exp. 2025-05-08) | **EXPIRED** |
| `draft-ietf-dult-finding` | **01** | 2025-06-06 (exp. 2025-12-08) | **EXPIRED** |
| `draft-ietf-dult-threat-model` | **05** | **2026-08-06** (exp. 2027-02-07), 49 pp | **ACTIVE** |

Threat-model authorship has moved off the vendors: **M. Delano (Swarthmore), J. Lowell (NNEDV), S. Prabhu (Nokia)**.

### Rationale / threat model (verbatim)

Accessory protocol abstract: *"…used to track their location **without their knowledge or consent**."*
§1.2: *"unwanted tracking alert refers to notifying the user of the presence of an unrecognized accessory that may be traveling with them over time."*
Threat model §1: *"An Attacker can **surreptitiously** plant an Accessory on a Target…"*; *"Frequent co-location of multiple Tags enables the Crowdsourced Network or a passive observer to infer social relationships, routines, or group behaviors, **compromising user privacy without consent**."*

**The doctrinal statement**, threat model §4.1.6 (aimed at Tile's Anti-Theft mode): *"**Any mode that makes it possible to turn off Unwanted Tracking Alerts is antithetical to DULT's recommendations**"* — alerts *"should not be circumventable under any circumstance."*

### Requirements that force physical visibility (accessory protocol -00)
- §3.13.3: sound maker **MUST** be present; **minimum 60 Phon peak loudness** (ISO 532-1:2017); MUST also sound when a non-owner triggers Play Sound.
- Table 17: `T_SEPARATED_UT_TIMEOUT` = **random 8–24 h uniform**; motion sampling **10 s**, dropping to **0.5 s** after motion; back-off **6 h** after 20 s motion or **10 sounds**. Accelerometer MUST detect ±10° on any two axes.
- §3.15: unique serial or privacy-preserving ID; BLE identifier retrieval **MUST require a physical action** (e.g. 10-second button hold).
- **§6 "Platform Support for Unwanted Tracking" is literally "TODO"** — the platform half was never written before the draft lapsed.

### The visibility granted is deliberately partial
*(Obfuscated) Owner Information*: *"no more than the last two digits of a phone number and/or an obfuscated email address with the first letter of the username and entity visible … (e.g., b\*\*\*\*\*@i\*\*\*\*.com)."* **The relation is made visible; the counterparty is not made enumerable.**

### Shipped
- **Android unknown tracker alerts**, **27 Jul 2023**, Erik Kay — https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/platforms/android/unknown-tracker-alert-google-android/ — Android 6.0+, AirTags only at launch. Google **held back its own Find My Device network** "until Apple has implemented protections for iOS."
- **Cross-platform**, **13 May 2024** — https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2024/05/apple-and-google-deliver-support-for-unwanted-tracking-alerts-in-ios-and-android/ — iOS 17.5 + Android 6.0+, "[Item] Found Moving With You." Committing manufacturers: **Chipolo, eufy, Jio, Motorola, Pebblebee**.
- **11 Dec 2024** — https://blog.google/feed/android-unknown-tracker-alerts/ — "Temporarily Pause Location" (≤24 h) and "Find Nearby."

---

## 2. AirTag alerts — history and MEASURED evaluation

**Apple, 10 Feb 2022** — https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2022/02/an-update-on-airtag-and-unwanted-tracking/ — promised setup privacy warnings stating tracking people without consent is criminal; AirPods-specific alert wording; Precision Finding for unknown AirTags (iPhone 11/12/13); display alert when an AirTag sounds; *"refined unwanted tracking alert logic to notify users earlier"*; louder tones. *"every AirTag has a unique serial number, and paired AirTags are associated with an Apple ID"* — supplied to law enforcement on subpoena. Apple's frequency claim: *"incidents of AirTag misuse are rare; however, each instance is one too many"* (CLAIMED, no denominator).

**Tracker Detect (Android)** released **13 Dec 2021** (date per Heinrich et al. 2022 §3). Play listing **NOT REACHED** (429/CAPTCHA).

**Current Apple page** https://support.apple.com/en-us/119874 — AirTag, AirPods (3rd gen/4/Pro/Max), Find My accessories; triggers when *"separated from its owner and seen moving with you over time"*; play sound, Precision Finding (UWB), NFC tap, disable instructions, detection map; DV hotlines US/UK/AU.

### Heinrich, Bittner, Hollick — "AirGuard", WiSec 2022, DOI 10.1145/3507657.3528546, arXiv:2202.11813

| Placement | AirGuard | iOS built-in |
|---|---|---|
| Pocket | **35 min** | **1 h 45 m 14 s**, only on return home; **OpenHaystack tag: never** |
| Backpack | **31 min** | **> 4 hours**, despite ≥70 linked locations; only on return home |
| Car (fuel cap) | 2nd ride / 3rd detection | **never**, not even on return home |

iOS *"delays notification until the user returns to their home location — which essentially gives the home location away to the attacker."* Self-built OpenHaystack tags never detected by either. Tags rotating keys >1×/hour defeat both.

Field deployment **15 Dec 2021 – 26 Jan 2022**: **>120,000 active users**, **>30,000** donating data, **>8,600,000** Find My advertisements, **>3,000,000** devices discovered (~**160,000** accessories). **2,494 notifications for 1,325 users.** Accessories = **4.1%** of devices found but **29.3%** of notifications. **97** user-marked false alarms (**85** from ordinary Apple devices). **~0.2% of data donors persistently "high risk"** (tracked >24 h, rolling 2-week window).

### Other measured work
- **Heinrich et al., PoPETs 2021(3):227–245**, DOI 10.2478/popets-2021-0045 — protocol analysis: unauthorised report access yields top-location recovery with **~10 m error in urban areas**; flaw exposing **past 7 days** of history.
- **Ibrahim et al., arXiv:2303.06073** — AirTag/SmartTag *"located **55% of the times in about 10 minutes within a 100 m radius**"*; concludes real-time precise stalking via tags is impractical.
- **Jang et al., arXiv:2501.17452** (29 Jan 2025) — **22 volunteers, 29 countries**; AirTag plateaus **79.8%** within-10 m after 60 min; SmartTag **54.8%** in 20 medium-density countries; Tile worse in Asia/Africa.
- **Mayberry et al., WPES 2021**, DOI 10.1145/3463676.3485616 — **full text NOT REACHED**; second-hand: iOS alert fires after the tag is seen in three separate locations within 24 h.
- **Briggs & Geeng, "BLE-Doubt", IEEE SPW 2022**, DOI 10.1109/spw54247.2022.9833870 — **full text NOT REACHED**.

### Police-report tally — Samantha Cole, Motherboard/VICE, **6 April 2022**
https://www.vice.com/en/article/apple-airtags-police-reports-stalking-harassment
- Requests to "dozens of the country's largest police departments"; **8 departments** responded; **150 AirTag-mentioning reports** over "a recent eight month period."
- **50** = women who received a tracking notification. **25** of those named a specific man (ex, husband, boss). **1 of 150** involved a man suspecting an ex-girlfriend. Rest: robberies, thefts, false alarms.
- *"AirTags have always implemented a notification feature that alerts people with iPhones … **anyone not using an iPhone or Apple device, however, would never know**."* On Tracker Detect: *"users have to learn about and then install the app, then allow it permission to work in the background—steps many people won't follow through with."*
- Counter-current: experts warn *"confronting an abusive partner or ex or disabling their tracking can be dangerous."*

### Litigation
- **Hughes v. Apple, Inc.**, N.D. Cal. **3:22-cv-07668**, filed **5 Dec 2022** (Lauren Hughes + Jane Doe; counsel Gillian Wade), Judge **Vince Chhabria**; **terminated in N.D. Cal. 2026-07-08**, transferred to C.D. Cal. as **2:26-cv-07457** (2026-07-09). 526 RECAP docs. https://www.courtlistener.com/docket/66613039/hughes-v-apple-inc/
- **IN RE: Apple AirTag Marketing, Sales Practices, and Products Liability Litigation, MDL No. 3199** (JPML), opened **4 Aug 2026**; Apple **opposed centralisation 25 Aug 2026**. Members incl. *Miller v. Apple* (W.D.N.C. 3:26-cv-00556, 9 Jul 2026), *Doe v. Apple* (D. Nev. 2:26-cv-02178, 15 Jul 2026), *Doe MP 1 v. Apple* (N.D. Fla. 3:26-cv-04937, 17 Aug 2026 — pseudonymity **denied** 19 Aug 2026).
- Only **11** published US opinions mention "AirTag"; earliest **2024-07-25** (*E.A. v. A.A.*, Ohio Ct. App.). State AG actions: **NOT REACHED**.

---

## 3. Covert vs. overt in the literature

**Chatterjee et al., IEEE S&P 2018**, DOI 10.1109/sp.2018.00061, https://rist.tech.cornell.edu/papers/spyware.pdf

Central finding cuts *against* a simple covert-is-worse frame: *"While we find dozens of overt spyware tools, **the majority are 'dual-use' apps**"*; *"Both overt spyware and dual-use apps are dangerous in IPV contexts."*

Covertness (§IV-B) is stated as attacker **motive**, not measured harm: *"In an IPS context, it's beneficial to an abuser if tools are covert, meaning they can operate without the victim's knowledge, and can potentially remain undiscovered even if the victim looks through all the apps in their app menu."*

Counts: 27,741 URLs; 1,629 active Play apps; **32** off-store apps; 9,224 apps crawled; 1,000 hand-labelled → **280 (28%) IPS-relevant**; **23** off-store spyware; **61 on-store + 9 off-store** installed and analysed; **8 of 11** vendor support teams responded affirmatively to an IPS request.

**The measured result is an enumerability failure.** Against 276 dual-use + 20 off-store + 100 benign apps:
- **37 of 40 anti-spyware tools flagged ≤3% of dual-use apps.**
- Best (Anti Spy Mobile): **95% of off-store spyware but only 47% of on-store IPS apps** — and it flagged Chrome.
- Major AV brands: **≤13%**. Via VirusTotal, **8%** of dual-use apps flagged by ≥3 engines.

On visible-but-useless signals: *"Even when notifications are present, we suspect victims are unlikely to observe them, let alone properly interpret their meaning"*; the Android GPS icon *"does not specify which app is using GPS, and is ever-present for many Android users."* Find My Device / Sprint Family Locator **do** notify, but *"notifications can be ignored or suppressed should the attacker have temporary physical access."* **A visible signal that does not name the relation is not enumerable.** Icon hiding: iKeyMonitor (reopened by dialling `#8888B`), TrackView (*"leaves no access point on the device once the icon is hidden"*), Cerberus.

**Roundy et al., IEEE S&P 2020**, DOI 10.1109/sp40000.2020.00069, https://rist.tech.cornell.edu/papers/creepware.pdf
- **~50 million Android devices**, billions of installs (Norton telemetry); CreepRank seeded with **18 covert surveillance apps** from Chatterjee et al.
- **857 of top-1,000** = creepware; **372** surveillance; **107 multifaceted surveillance apps affected 172K Norton customers in 2017 alone**; >1M creepware installs; **115** spoofing apps (41 burner-phone). Fleiss' κ = **0.77** / **0.75**.
- Invisibility as an ecosystem: categories **Control – Hide-Icon** and **Evasion – Hidden-Content**. Seeding with 9 icon-hiders returned **12 app-hiders** in the top 50; three hidden apps *"can only be opened by calling a fake phone number."* Countermarket exists: *"'Hidden Apps', a defensive app that reveals the presence of hidden apps"* — **enumerability is the market's own countermeasure.**
- Surveillance is coded as apps that *"**both covertly and overtly** track someone's location"* — harm is **not** separated by covertness.

**Tseng et al., USENIX Security 2020**, https://www.usenix.org/system/files/sec20-tseng.pdf
- **>200,000 posts / ~20,000 threads**, 5 forums; **750 threads** coded (250 each × 3). One forum: **78%** of sampled threads IPS-related. Forum C ≈ 80,000 subscribers (Feb 2020).
- Taxonomy splits by **physical access**, not covertness: tool-based requiring access (spyware, keyloggers, GPS on body/car, A/V recorders) vs. not requiring it (**shared phone plan**, **shared cloud tools**, router monitoring, reverse lookup), plus coercion/subterfuge and hiring a PI.
- **The word "covert" appears once in the whole paper. It does not claim attackers preferred covert tools.** What it shows is attackers being steered to **legitimate, account-level, relational** access.

**Freed et al., CHI 2018**, DOI 10.1145/3173574.3174241, mirror https://www.nixdell.com/papers/stalkers-paradise-intimate.pdf — qualitative, **N=89** (11 focus groups / **39 survivors** + **50 professionals**); password coercion reported by **n=28**. Core construct: the **UI-bound adversary** — *"an adversarial but authenticated user that interacts with a victim's device or account via standard user interfaces."* By construction **not covert malware**.

> ⚠️ **Defect found in a live standards document.** `draft-ietf-dult-threat-model-05` §3.2.2 attributes to Freed et al. 2018 the figures **72% / 41% / 67%** (coerced password sharing; device rifling; remote account "hacking"). **The Freed et al. paper contains zero "%" characters and reports no percentages** — it is qualitative, N=89, n=28 on password coercion. Misattributed in the active IETF WG document; true source **NOT IDENTIFIED**.

---

## 4. Coercive control law and the evidentiary role of monitoring

**Serious Crime Act 2015 s.76** — https://www.legislation.gov.uk/ukpga/2015/9/section/76 — in force **29 Dec 2015**. Repeated/continuous controlling or coercive behaviour toward a personally connected person with "serious effect" = fear of violence on **≥2 occasions**, or serious alarm/distress with a **substantial adverse effect on B's usual day-to-day activities**; A knows or ought to know. Defence: best-interests + reasonable, unavailable where the effect is fear of violence. Max **5 years** on indictment.

**CPS guidance** — https://www.cps.gov.uk/legal-guidance/controlling-or-coercive-behaviour-intimate-or-family-relationship (updated **24 Apr 2023** and **7 Feb 2025**):
- Conduct list includes *"monitoring their time"* and *"**monitoring a person via online communication tools or using spyware**."*
- **The decisive policy sentence:** *"**A victim may not know the full extent of a suspect's conduct, for instance if they were being monitored without their knowledge.**"*
- Evidential list includes *"phone records, text messages and device logs"*, *"evidence of abuse over the internet, digital technology (e.g. smart speakers) and social media platforms"*, and *"**GPS tracking devices installed covertly an/or overtly on mobile phones, tablets, vehicles etc**"* — covert and overt treated as **equally evidential**, not differently severe.

**Home Office statutory guidance framework** — first published 5 Dec 2015; current version **5 Apr 2023**, updated **27 Jul 2023**: https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/controlling-or-coercive-behaviour-statutory-guidance-framework (PDF: https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/642d3f9e7de82b001231364d/Controlling_or_Coercive_Behaviour_Statutory_Guidance_-_final.pdf).

**Domestic Abuse (Scotland) Act 2018 asp 5** — https://www.legislation.gov.uk/asp/2018/5/section/2 — in force **1 Apr 2019** (SSI 2018/387). s.2(3) "relevant effects" include **(c) "controlling, regulating or monitoring B's day-to-day activities."** Monitoring is a **named statutory element**, with **no covert/overt distinction**.

**Prosecution statistics — Home Office, "Review of the controlling or coercive behaviour offence," 10 May 2021**
https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/608bdba28fa8f51b909ca167/review-of-the-controlling-or-coercive-behaviour-offence.pdf

| Metric | Figure |
|---|---|
| Police-recorded CCB offences 2016/17 | **4,246** |
| Police-recorded CCB offences 2019/20 | **24,856** |
| Defendants prosecuted, CCB as principal offence, 2019 | **1,112** |
| Charge rate | **11% (2017/18) → 6% (2018/19)** |
| Closed for evidential difficulties, 2018/19 | **85%** |
| Insufficient evidence *despite victim support*, 2018/19 | **35%** CCB vs **24%** all DA vs **33%** DA-stalking |
| Conviction ratio (principal offence) | **38% (2016)**, rising |
| Out-of-court disposals | **~0%** (vs 3% all DA) |
| FoI, first 18 months (McClenaghan & Boutard 2017) | **532 charges / 29 forces**; **6 forces ≤5 charges** |

Diagnosis: *"evidencing CCB is a significant challenge for police and prosecutors, likely due to the nature of CCB as a course of conduct offence that often includes non-physical abuse."* Barlow et al. (2019), quoted: officers pursued "photographic evidence" of one assault *"with many of the woman's descriptions of coercive control being disregarded as examples of 'one word against the other', and thus 'weak' or 'unverifiable'."*

**So prosecution does turn on discoverable artefacts, and the record quantifies the failure when artefacts are absent — 6% charge rate, 85% evidential closures. But no source isolates "the tooling left no trace" as a distinct measured cause.**

**NOT REACHED:** s.76 counts by year after 2019/20 (ONS *Domestic abuse and the criminal justice system*, latest **26 Nov 2025**, does **not** disaggregate CCB; CPS quarterly summaries do not either). No measured evaluation of the 2023 statutory guidance.

---

## 5. Third-party enumerability

**Havron et al., USENIX Security 2019**, https://www.usenix.org/system/files/sec19-havron.pdf — ISDi + TAQ + technograph, deployed in NYC Family Justice Centers. **N = 44 clients.**

- **23/44 (52%)** had identified risks; **21/44** nothing surfaced (19 relieved; **2 left unsatisfied, no explanation found even after outside expert follow-up**).

| Finding | Count |
|---|---|
| Potential **spyware** | **3** |
| Password compromise | **14** |
| **Unknown "trusted" devices** | **12** |
| Shared family/phone plan | **4** |
| Rooted device | **1** |

- ISDi flagged **79** apps: **61 dual-use** ("find my phone", child monitoring most prevalent) — for **all but one**, clients recognised them; **18** false positives; max **5** flags on any one device.
- Authors' explanation for the low spyware rate: *"many abusers are seemingly able to surveil clients via compromised accounts, and so may not need to install spyware."*
- **The relations that mattered were found by enumeration, not scanning.** Manual iCloud checks: **4** clients had unrecognised/abuser "trusted" devices; account logs: **8** more with unknown/abuser logins. **2** clients had unrecognised browser extensions (one sideloaded off-store, read/write on all browser data).
- Non-enumerable chains as safety cost: **3** clients synced password files to a compromised iCloud; several emailed new passwords to compromised addresses; clients used compromised accounts as recovery accounts *"unaware of how this might result in abuser access to these services."*

**Tseng et al. 2020** — the attacker side of the same surface: *"attackers are encouraged by the forum to use their partner's Apple ID to view their personal messages and photos from a web browser—no device access necessary"*; a family iPad synced to a partner's iCloud; a three-party shared iCloud exposing an affair partner's purchase/browsing history; Google Maps Timeline; iOS Significant Locations; WhatsApp Web pairing as *"a one-minute job"* best done while a partner sleeps, the poster *"actually shocked at what a privacy flaw this seems to be."*

**Industry answer:** Apple **Safety Check** (iOS 16+, Sept 2022) — https://support.apple.com/guide/personal-safety/safety-check-iphone-ios-16-ips2aad835e1/web — *Emergency Reset* "quickly stop sharing with all people and apps"; *Manage Sharing & Access* reviews information shared with **people**, with **apps**, and "review and remove **devices** connected to your Apple Account," plus verification numbers and emergency contacts. **This is a first-party enumerator for the device owner only.** I found **no** mechanism and **no** measured evaluation giving an **advocate, clinician or court** third-party enumeration — the ISDi clinic model works only with the client present and their device over USB.

**DULT concedes the logs do not exist** (threat model §4.1.5): a successful protocol *"would include a log file that is clearly labeled and easily accessible … that is not easily edited"* recording *"the account, the Device, GPS location, and the time"* — and *"**Logs should not be easily editable to avoid gaslighting and accountability efforts**."* Ideally also a log of when the owner checked the tag's location while near the target — acknowledged as requiring network cooperation and **unbuilt**. §4.1.1: *"a Target's ability to mitigate threats to their safety depends on consumer choices and access"*, and platform differences *"can also be exploited by an Attacker familiar with target Platforms."*

---

## 6. Countervailing evidence

1. **The IETF's own threat model says visible defence can escalate danger.** §3.1: *"**taking overt security measures while frequently in physical proximity to the Attacker may lead to the Attacker escalating their tactics of interpersonal control.** Risk assessments and the weighing of tradeoffs in such situations are often highly individualized."* VICE reports the same.

2. **Alerts carry measured false-positive/fatigue cost.** DULT §4.1.2: *"Some people may develop 'alert fatigue' from false positives … [some] decrease/disable sensitivity because alerts cause anxiety or false positives condition them to ignore alerts."* AirGuard: 97 false alarms in six weeks, 85 from ordinary Apple devices.

3. **Detectability can be turned against the target.** DULT §3.2: existing measures *"have been criticized as potentially making it easier for an Attacker to track a Target using the **Target's own Tag**"* if identifiers rotate too slowly. Cited mitigation: https://eprint.iacr.org/2023/1332.pdf.

4. **The only quantitative visible-vs-invisible test found — and it is only partly supportive.** Ravid, White, Tomczak, Miles & Behrend, *Personnel Psychology*, DOI **10.1111/peps.12514** (25 Apr 2022); preprint https://osf.io/ef43u/. **K = 94 samples, N = 23,461.**
   - *"Results provide no evidence that EPM improves worker performance."*
   - *"**the presence of EPM increases worker stress and strain, regardless of the characteristics of monitoring**."*
   - Transparency → attitudes overall ρ = **.25** [.11, .39]; fairness/justice ρ = **.21** [.04, .38]; commitment ρ = **.35** [.10, .61]; perceived support ρ = **.37** [.13, .62]; satisfaction ρ = **.26** [.08, .44].
   - Transparency → **no effect on performance** (H3a unsupported). **H3c (transparency → stress/strain) could not be tested: K = 2.**
   - Invasiveness moderated attitudes negatively; *"No observed relationship in our meta-analysis was stronger than the relationship between **person-targeted EPM and perceptions of privacy invasion**."*
   - Authors' forward claim, offered as reasoning not result: *"Transparency becomes even more important as monitoring technologies become increasingly discreet and unobtrusive, as there is more opportunity to conceal what is being tracked."*

   **Read plainly: making monitoring visible measurably improved how people felt about the monitor. It did not measurably reduce the harm.**

---

## Verdict table

| Claim | Status |
|---|---|
| A tracked person MUST be alerted; alerts must not be circumventable | **POLICY / SPEC.** Apple + Google + 5 vendors (2023), + 5 more (2024); IETF WG chartered 2024-02-29 with an NNEDV co-chair; **shipped** 13 May 2024. **Not standardised** — no RFC, both protocol drafts expired, §6 "Platform Support" still "TODO" at lapse. |
| Attackers *prefer* covert tools | **ASSERTED with a stated motive** (Chatterjee 2018 §IV-B), **never counted.** Tseng 2020 uses "covert" once; Roundy 2020 codes covert and overt together. |
| Covert tooling is *harder to enumerate* | **MEASURED, strongly.** 37/40 anti-spyware tools flag ≤3% of dual-use apps; major AV ≤13%; 8% via ≥3 VirusTotal engines. Icon-hiding is a distinct market with its own counter-market. |
| The dangerous relations are covert malware | **REFUTED by field data.** Havron 2019: **3** spyware findings vs **12** unknown trusted devices, **14** password compromises, **4** shared plans (N=44). Freed 2018's central construct is the **UI-bound adversary** — a legitimate, authenticated, frequently *visible* relation. |
| Prosecution turns on discoverable evidence of monitoring | **YES, measured in aggregate:** 6% charge rate, 85% evidential closures, 35% insufficient-evidence with a supporting victim. CPS 2023/25 states it directly. **No source isolates "traceless tooling" as a measured cause.** |
| **Invisible management is more dangerous than visible management** | **UNTESTED as stated.** No study compares the same relation covert vs. disclosed on a harm outcome. Nearest quantitative test (K=94, N=23,461) finds transparency improves **attitudes** (ρ≈.25), **not performance**, and **could not test stress/strain (K=2)** — stress rose under monitoring **regardless of characteristics**. The IETF's own threat model asserts the opposite in one specific case: **overt** defensive measures near an attacker can escalate. |

### NOT REACHED
Google's May-2023 DULT post · Google Play / support.google.com (429 + CAPTCHA) · Apple newsroom post for Tracker Detect (404; likely never existed) · Mayberry et al. WPES 2021 full text · Briggs & Geeng "BLE-Doubt" full text · s.76 counts by year 2020/21–2025/26 · any evaluation of the 2023 Home Office guidance · state AG actions on AirTag stalking · OpenAlex (daily budget exhausted mid-session; switched to Crossref + arXiv + CourtListener).
